# 12. Supervisor restart

You wrote:

```bash
supervisorctl restart fastapi
```

✅ Excellent.

This is realistic for your architecture.

The deployment flow becomes:

```text
New code
 ↓
EC2
 ↓
Supervisor restart
 ↓
FastAPI
```

---

# 13. Supervisor status

```bash
supervisorctl status fastapi
```

✅ Excellent.

This is better than assuming:

> Restart command succeeded = application is working.

You are checking actual process state.

---

# 14. Health check

You wrote:

```text
curl -I v https:fastapi
```

Syntax needs correction, but concept is **excellent**.

The important thing is:

```text
Deployment
    ↓
Process status
    ↓
HTTP health check
```

For example conceptually:

```bash
curl -f https://dev-api.example.com/health
```

`-f` is useful because a failing HTTP response can cause the command to fail.

If your application doesn't have `/health`, use whatever endpoint is actually appropriate.

---

# 15. Deployment completed

You wrote:

```text
Deployment completed successfully.
echo: Deployment Done.
```

Concept is correct.

But understand:

If the previous commands fail, you don't want:

```text
Deployment Done
```

to execute anyway.

You want:

```text
Install ❌
     ↓
Workflow FAILED
```

or:

```text
Health check ❌
     ↓
Workflow FAILED
```

And only:

```text
Health check ✅
     ↓
Deployment successful
```

This is a **very important CI/CD principle**.

